<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2"><Document><name>Rome guide 14 Nov</name>
<Placemark><name>1. Aqueduct arches: water for the palace</name><description>Brick facing over a concrete core. Small square holes in the brickwork where scaffolding beams once sat. The line of arches heading east toward the Caelian Hill.</description><Point><coordinates>12.4886,41.88745,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>2. Circus Maximus viewpoint (core)</name><description>The oval outline of the valley. The curved end at the far southeast, with a small medieval tower. The Aventine Hill rising on the other side.</description><Point><coordinates>12.4864,41.887,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>3. Stadium of Domitian</name><description>The curved recess on the long side. The bases of the colonnade's piers. Brick walls that once carried marble.</description><Point><coordinates>12.487,41.88805,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>4. Palace of Domitian (core)</name><description>Huge brick walls with rows of small holes, where marble panels were fixed. In the private wing, the sunken courtyard with its fountain basin. In the dining hall, the small brick pillars that held up a heated floor.</description><Point><coordinates>12.48575,41.88875,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>5. Romulus's hilltop</name><description>Post holes in the rock, if the shelter is open. The platform of the Temple of Magna Mater nearby. The view west toward the Tiber, where the first river crossing made this hill valuable.</description><Point><coordinates>12.4839,41.8887,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>6. Farnese Gardens terrace: the Forum below (core)</name><description>The line of the Via Sacra. The three columns of Castor and Pollux. The rectangular footprint of the Basilica Julia, marked by column stumps.</description><Point><coordinates>12.4854,41.89065,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>7. Arch of Titus (core)</name><description>The menorah relief inside the passage. The coffered vault with the eagle. The colour change between the original marble centre and Valadier's travertine sides.</description><Point><coordinates>12.48838,41.89065,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>8. Basilica of Maxentius (core)</name><description>The octagonal coffers in the vaults. The thickness of the piers. Stubs of the collapsed central vault at the top of the walls.</description><Point><coordinates>12.4886,41.89175,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>9. Temple of Vesta and House of the Vestals (core)</name><description>The partly rebuilt round temple. The long pools in the courtyard. The statues of chief Vestals, and the base with the erased name.</description><Point><coordinates>12.48625,41.8916,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>10. Temple of Caesar (core)</name><description>The curved niche in the front wall, where an altar marked the spot of the cremation. Flowers left by visitors.</description><Point><coordinates>12.48632,41.89205,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>11. Basilica Julia (core)</name><description>Game boards scratched into the steps and floor: look for circles with spokes and small grids. The line of pillar stumps showing the hall's aisles.</description><Point><coordinates>12.48515,41.89205,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>12. Temple of Saturn</name><description>Columns and bases that do not quite match: evidence of late repairs with reused pieces. The restoration inscription on the frieze.</description><Point><coordinates>12.48455,41.8924,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>13. Rostra (core)</name><description>Holes along the platform's front where the ship rams were fixed. The brick stump of the Umbilicus Urbis. Fragments of the Golden Milestone near the Temple of Saturn.</description><Point><coordinates>12.48492,41.89262,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>14. Arch of Septimius Severus</name><description>The fourth line of the inscription, where the holes do not match the letters. The worn relief panels showing the Parthian campaigns.</description><Point><coordinates>12.48485,41.89287,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>15. Lapis Niger</name><description>The black paving inside its low enclosure. The shrine underneath is usually closed.</description><Point><coordinates>12.4852,41.89295,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>16. Curia Julia, the Senate House (core)</name><description>The brick facade, once covered in stucco made to look like marble. The bronze doors, today modern copies. If the interior is open: the tiers where the senators' benches stood.</description><Point><coordinates>12.48535,41.8931,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>17. Shrine of Venus Cloacina and Basilica Aemilia</name><description>The small round base with its low railing. Green stains on the floor of the Basilica Aemilia, visible from the walkway.</description><Point><coordinates>12.4859,41.89275,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>18. Temple of Antoninus and Faustina (exit here)</name><description>The top inscription line naming Antoninus. The church door high above the temple steps. The grooves at the tops of the columns.</description><Point><coordinates>12.487,41.89252,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>19. Outside: Nero's lake becomes a people's arena (core)</name><description>The full four-storey outer wall on the north side, and the lower, broken south side. The two big sloping brick buttresses at the ends of the surviving outer wall, added in the early 1800s to stop it collapsing.</description><Point><coordinates>12.492,41.8909,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>20. Arch of Constantine</name><description>The eight attic panels from Marcus Aurelius's monument. The round hunting scenes from Hadrian's time. The narrow 4th-century frieze above the side arches.</description><Point><coordinates>12.49065,41.88982,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>21. The shell: how it stands (core)</name><description>Clamp holes in the travertine. The change from travertine piers to brick vaults. The ring corridors. Roman numerals above the arches, visible from outside on the north side.</description><Point><coordinates>12.4919,41.8904,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>22. The seating: a map of Roman society (core)</name><description>The level of the podium, just above the arena. Where the emperor's box would have been, on the middle of a long side. Names carved into surviving marble seat blocks.</description><Point><coordinates>12.49215,41.8901,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>23. The underground, seen from above (core)</name><description>The long central corridor. Rows of slots and niches in the walls, where lift shafts ran. The partly rebuilt arena floor at one end, which shows the original level.</description><Point><coordinates>12.4925,41.89025,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>24. The velarium: shade for 50,000</name><description>The brackets and mast sockets at the top of the outer wall, best seen from outside on the north side. The stone posts around the pavement on the east side.</description><Point><coordinates>12.4924,41.8905,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>25. Level 2 and the museum: the political deal</name><description>Spectator graffiti in the museum. The view west to the Arch of Titus, the Temple of Venus and Rome, and the Palatine.</description><Point><coordinates>12.4917,41.8903,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Palatine entrance (Via di San Gregorio 30)</name><description></description><Point><coordinates>12.48946,41.88833,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Forum exit (Largo della Salara Vecchia)</name><description></description><Point><coordinates>12.48745,41.89318,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Lunch: Via Cavour 215</name><description></description><Point><coordinates>12.49202,41.89445,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Colosseum security</name><description></description><Point><coordinates>12.49175,41.89075,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
